erts believe a pandemic welfare programme for poorer Brazilians has encouraged robbers to plan bold raids in sleepy regional cities where bank branches are storing more cash.", "reference": "2021 August 30, “Armed robbers take hostages in deadly bank raids in Brazil city”, in Katharine Viner, editor, The Guardian, London: Guardian News & Media, →ISSN, →OCLC:"}, {"text": "Usually sleepy border crossings into Kazakhstan and Mongolia have also been overwhelmed by the sudden influx of Russians looking for a way out.", "reference": "2022 September 23, Pjotr Sauer, Dan Sabbagh, “Border queues build as people flee Russia to escape Putin's call-up”, in Katharine Viner, editor, The Guardian, London: Guardian News & Media, →ISSN, →OCLC:"}, {"text": "Despite Horwich's international fame as a centre of railway engineering, Horwich station itself was usually quite a sleepy place.", "reference": "2023 March 8, Paul Salveson, “Fond farewells to two final trains...”, in Rail, number 978, page 54:"}, {"text": "The meeting has put Pakistan at the center of the biggest story in the world, spotlighting its normally sleepy capital that has been preparing for days for high-profile delegations to arrive.", "reference": "2026 April 12, Betsy Joles, “No Deal: U.S.-Iran peace talks in Islamabad fall through”, in National Public Radio, Washington, D.C.: National Public Radio:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [{"language": {"code": "bg", "name": "Bulgarian"}, "word": "заспа́л"}, {"language": {"code": "fi", "name": "Finnish"}, "word": "unelias"}, {"language": {"code": "fi", "name": "Finnish"}, "word": "hiljainen"}, {"language": {"code": "gl", "name": "Galician"}, "word": "durmiñento"}, {"language": {"code": "de", "name": "German"}, "word": "verschlafen"}, {"language": {"code": "ga", "name": "Irish"}, "word": "meirbh"}], "subsenses": []}], "synonyms": ["adrowse", "bedroomy", "dozy", "dopey", "dragging one's tail", "drowsy", "eyelids at half-mast", "nodding off", "oscitant", "o